description.narrative:
Since mid-October 2023, International Alert has started this second phase of the "MUPAKA SHAMBA LETU: the border, our livelihood" project. This aims to strengthen peace and stability in the Great Lakes region by focusing on the scale up of the previous phase focus on women small scale cross-border trade and improving key aspects of the project to ensure ownership and sustainability. 

The MSL II project has significantly impacted women small-scale cross-border traders by enhancing their governance, financial autonomy, and conflict management skills. It has promoted the inclusion of women and youth and strengthened economic and customs services as part of the DRC's integration into the EAC beyond the COMESA simplified trade regime (STR). The project has contributed to sustainable economic growth and social cohesion in grassroot in the Great Lakes region through targeted training, advocacy, and innovative economic initiatives.

Key achievements include:

- Small-scale cross-border traders have been identified and registered in a single database of 6,681 traders and 1,159 young entrepreneurs. For the inclusion of Twas (Pygmies) in Burundi among the beneficiaries during the first phase;
- Transparent and inclusive partner selection, involving five Civil Society Organisations (CSOs), five Platforms of Cross-Border Traders' Associations (P-ACT), and two Private Sector Federations.
- Empowerment of women and young people, with 6,681 traders and 1,159 young entrepreneurs registered in a database.
- The training and capacity-building sessions on transformational leadership and governance, financial management and accountability, and structuring and running the AVECs (Village Savings and Credit Association) reached 454 beneficiaries, including 344 WCBT leaders and 110 young entrepreneurs, with an overall progress rate 2 of 72% (including 80% in Burundi and 58% in the DRC). And as part of the drive to make P-ACT (Cross Border Trade Association Platform) more self-sufficient and provide sustainable, high-quality services to WCBTs, the P-ACT in the DRC and Burundi trained 261 beneficiaries, including 19 young entrepreneurs, 235 WCBTs and seven Platform Leaders.
- Capacity-building sessions reaching 454 beneficiaries, including Women Cross Border Traders leaders and young entrepreneurs;
- Following training in the financial education programme, 10 trainers have been certified and were now able to supervise other multipliers. 72 multipliers have been trained and certified, enabling them to disseminate the knowledge they have acquired in their communities and preventing embezzlement and harassment;
- Reorganization and professionalization of cross-border trader associations, enhancing transparency and autonomy;

- The MSL II project supported entrepreneurial initiatives and financial empowerment by organizing a business plan competition in which 11 cooperatives were rewarded for their innovative ideas, with a total prize of $29,610. The prize money benefited 555 members of these cooperatives (284 in Rwanda and 271 in the DRC): Local production of soya and maize flour (Uvira, DRC): boosting food self-sufficiency and reducing dependence on imports. Local production of poultry feed (Goma, DRC): reducing production costs and empowering farmers. Floating cage fish farming (Rwanda): improving local nutrition and creating jobs; 

Overall, the MSL II project has fostered structured and inclusive cross-border trade to become a vector for peace and sustainable development in the region.
